## Why This Is Asked

This question probes experience with crash-consistent in-memory structures, per-core data locality, and durable metadata synchronization under power failures—critical in low-latency financial/ads platforms.

## Key Concepts

- Per-core log-structured index and Bloom filter for fast lookups
- Durable append order via epoch fences and fsync/msync semantics
- Recovery by replaying per-core logs in a deterministic order
- False sharing minimization and memory-mapped file durability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo replay of per-core logs to rebuild index
function replay_logs(coreLogs) {
  for (const core of coreLogs) {
    for (const entry of core.segments) {
      apply(entry);
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How do you handle Bloom filter false positives during recovery?
- What are the failure modes during corruption of a core log segment, and how do you detect them?
- How would you validate performance under steady-state and crash-recovery cycles?
